(* ============================================================================
   18. MATH -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-036] (governs `math` in PART 3; djot form). Inline
      `$` + verbatim backtick span; display `$$` + verbatim backtick
      span. A `$` NOT immediately followed by a backtick run is literal
      (`$5` is currency); `\$` escapes a literal `$`. Rendering:
        inline  -> <span class="math inline" role="math">\(content\)</span>
        display -> <span class="math display" role="math">\[content\]</span>
      `content` is the verbatim span text, HTML-escaped (`&`,`<`,`>`).
      Display math alone on a line renders inside its own paragraph. A
      standalone display-math block carrying a trailing caption (PART 9 §4)
      is a numbered EQUATION: the math paragraph is wrapped in a figure and a
      `</#id>` to it resolves to "Equation N" (§19).
      Author `{attrs}` after the span merge onto the <span> (the base
      `math …` class is kept; see PART 10 §1).

      A directly numbered display equation renders its label and number after
      the math span as one U+0020 followed by
      `<span class="equation-number">LABEL NUMBER</span>`, with one U+0020
      between LABEL and NUMBER and no brackets. `attrs.id` remains on the math
      span, so a resolved cross-reference lands on the formula. Plain, ANSI and
      Markdown append one U+0020 followed by the same `LABEL NUMBER` text, with
      no brackets, under their ordinary inline escaping rules.

      A MATH SPAN CARRIES ROLE MATH -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-037].
      The renderer adds `role="math"` even without a typesetter.
      THE NAME STAYS THE AUTHOR'S: a trailing `aria-label` supplies the
      formula's spoken form; no §16a key is added. An authored `role` wins
      under any ASCII casing (SS12). Generated `role` is written LAST, after
      merged author attributes, without moving them (PART 10 §1).

      AN IMPORTER WRITES THE CORE FORM, NEVER THE EXTENSION FENCE --
      NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-038]. `<div class="math display">` imports as `$$`
      plus a verbatim backtick span in a paragraph holding a `math` node;
      `<span class="math inline">` imports as the inline `$` form.
      THE REASON IS THAT AN IMPORTER MUST NOT EMIT A CONSTRUCT WHOSE MEANING
      DEPENDS ON THE CONSUMER'S CONFIGURATION: the ```` ```math ```` extension
      fence degrades to `language-math` code when the extension is absent.
      THE ROUND-TRIP ARGUMENT IS REAL AND LOSES ANYWAY: even if that extension
      generated the HTML, the importer MUST emit the same core form regardless
      of whether an extension is registered.
      THE MATHML PATH WRITES NO TRAILING DELIMITER. A `<math>` read through
      `alttext` writes `` $`E = mc^2` `` inline or ``` $$`E = mc^2` ``` for
      `display="block"`. The closing backtick ends the construct; a trailing
      `$` or `$$` would be literal text.

   19. CROSSREF AUTO-TEXT + DEFAULT-ON / PROCESSOR FEATURES -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-039]
      (resolution formalized as PART 9R rules R4/R5; behavior detail here).
      - `</#id>` clones the target heading's full inline children, so a
        heading with markup (`# *Setup*`) yields a link that KEEPS the
        markup (`<a href="#…"><strong>Setup</strong> …</a>`).
      - A `</#id>` cross-reference resolves to exactly ONE LEVEL: it links
        to the target and adopts the target's text, FLATTENING any nested
        cross-reference in that text (a `</#…>` in the cloned heading text is
        not re-expanded). One-level resolution makes a self-reference
        (`# A </#a>`) and a mutual cycle (`# A </#b>` + `# B </#a>`) SAFE --
        each side resolves once, no infinite expansion. A normal, non-cyclic
        crossref still resolves. The corpus pins this (resources/examples/edge-cases.md
        "Cyclic cross-reference resolves to one level").
      - `</#id>` to a NUMBERED CAPTION (a figure / table / listing / equation
        whose caption carries a number placeholder and an `{#id}`) yields the
        caption's LABEL + NUMBER ("Figure 1"), markup preserved, NOT the
        caption prose. A directly numbered display equation resolves through
        its `attrs.id` and yields its string LABEL + NUMBER ("Equation 1"). A
        `</#id>` to an id that is neither a heading nor either numbered target
        stays unresolved and renders literally.
      - Mentions (`@user`), tags (`#tag`) and smart typography are ON by
        default in the conformant core; a processor MAY disable them. The
        corpus pins the default-on behavior.
      - SOCIAL LINK RESOLUTION -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-070]. Mention and tag
        syntax is core; turning either node into a link is opt-in processor
        configuration. A host MAY supply a MENTION resolver, a TAG resolver,
        or both. For each parsed node of the configured kind, the processor
        gives the resolver the node's kind (`mention` or `tag`), its exact
        parsed name, its AST attributes, and an opaque read-only host context.
        The resolver returns ONE complete link destination or UNRESOLVED. An
        implementation MAY expose this as a synchronous or asynchronous API;
        the observable result is the same.

        THE RESULT CHANGES ONLY THE DESTINATION. A resolved mention or tag is
        rendered through the same link form as a configured URL template. Its
        visible label remains the authored `@name` or `#name`; its structural
        `mention` / `tag` class and AST attributes remain governed by PART 3
        and PART 10. A resolver cannot return HTML, replacement inline content,
        classes, attributes, or another Carve node. Its result is never parsed
        as Carve and never starts another resolution pass.

        RESOLVER PRECEDENCE IS PER KIND. When a resolver is configured for a
        kind, it is authoritative for that kind: a returned UNRESOLVED value or
        an error renders the ordinary inert `<span>` and MUST NOT fall through
        to that kind's URL template. A processor SHOULD report a resolver error
        as a diagnostic, but MUST NOT fail the whole render solely because one
        social token did not resolve. When no resolver is configured for the
        kind, the existing `mentionUrl` / `tagUrl` template behavior applies.
        When neither mechanism is configured, the node remains an inert span.

        A TEMPLATE receives a percent-encoded name before substitution. A
        resolver receives the exact parsed name and returns a complete
        destination, so the processor MUST NOT encode or interpolate that
        result a second time. Both template and resolver destinations MUST pass
        the PART 9 section 25 URL-scheme denylist; a denied destination is
        UNRESOLVED and renders the inert span. The callback is host code, not a
        permission to perform network or database access: a host that does so
        owns authentication, authorization, timeouts, request bounds, and
        tenant containment. The processor invokes at most one configured
        social resolver per parsed node and MUST NOT rescan a returned value.
      - FILE INCLUSION / TRANSCLUSION (`{{ … }}`) is a PROCESSOR-LEVEL
        directive, NOT part of the core parser. Its syntax is PART 6; its
        normative semantics are below and mirrored, with worked examples, in
        docs/includes.md. A conformant CORE never recognizes the directive
        (unreachable from `block`/`inline`) and leaves `{{ … }}` LITERAL --
        it parses the bytes under ordinary inline rules, so a bare-path
        directive with no active inline markers emits verbatim (corpus
        "Include directive with no resolver renders literal"). The rules a
        conforming PROCESSOR that DOES expand includes MUST follow:

        I1 SYNTAX. `{{ path }}`, `{{ path #section }}`, `{{ path @key:value }}`,
           combinable `{{ path #section @key:value }}` (PART 6). `path` is bare
           (stops at space / `#` / `@` / `}`) or double-quoted for spaces, and
           is resolved RELATIVE TO THE INCLUDING FILE. Options split into two
           disjoint kinds. SELECTION options choose WHICH content is pulled in:
           `#section` (semantic -- a heading's section or one block, I1a) and
           `@lines:N-M` (physical -- a 1-based
           inclusive line range). The two SELECTION mechanisms are MUTUALLY
           EXCLUSIVE: a directive MUST select by `#section` OR by a line-range,
           NEVER both; a directive carrying both is an ERROR (Warning + literal,
           per I7). TRANSFORM options reshape already-selected content and are
           orthogonal to selection: `@shift:N` shifts every included heading's
           level by the SIGNED integer N (I8). `@shift` MAY accompany `#section`,
           a line-range, or neither. `@shift` takes EITHER a signed integer OR
           the literal value `auto` (context-relative, computed by the processor
           per I8), never both. Every other `@key` is RESERVED.

        I1a FRAGMENT SELECTION (`#name`). `name` is an `explicit_identifier`
           (PART 6), so every explicit id is nameable, digit-leading ones such
           as `{#2024-plan}` included. It is matched the way a `</#id>`
           cross-reference matches (R4), against the child's OWN parse, before
           the child's nested includes expand. Two steps, the first that
           matches wins:
           (1) HEADING. A heading whose id (explicit `{#id}` or auto slug) is
               `name` selects its SECTION: that heading and the sibling blocks
               after it in the same block sequence, up to the next heading of
               the same or higher level or the end of that sequence. A heading
               nested in a container is bounded by its container.
           (2) BLOCK. Otherwise a BLOCK carrying the EXPLICIT id `name`
               selects exactly that block, with its own attributes. A block is
               a `blockNode` (PART 12) standing in a block sequence of the
               child's body, at any depth, other than a definition (footnote,
               reference, abbreviation, citation), a comment or frontmatter.
           Within a step, the FIRST match in document order wins (a container
           precedes its descendants). An id carried only by something that is
           not a block in the body -- an inline element, a list item, a table
           row or cell, a definition term, a footnote definition or a block
           inside one -- selects NOTHING: the directive is an ERROR (Warning +
           literal, per I7), as for an id the child does not contain. Every
           other rule treats a block selection as it treats a section: I2 in
           inline position (a selected paragraph splices its inlines and its
           own attributes do not carry into the sentence; any other block is
           block-in-inline), I4 provenance, I5, I6, I8 (T is measured over the
           selected block; no headings in it makes `auto` a no-op), I11 (a
           missing id leaves the dependency RESOLVED) and I13.

        I2 BLOCK vs INLINE. A directive ALONE on its own line is a BLOCK
           include: the resolved content merges as BLOCKS at the directive
           site. A directive within inline content is an INLINE include: the
           resolved content MUST parse (as an I4 fragment) to INLINE-ONLY
           content -- a single paragraph, or nothing -- and its inlines are
           spliced into the surrounding inline sequence. Resolved content
           with any other block structure in inline position is an ERROR
           (Warning + literal, per I7). Mirrors the §10 block/inline model.

        I3 RESOLUTION MODEL. The CORE performs NO file I/O. The HOST supplies a
           resolver with the contract: given (path, section?, options?,
           includingFileContext) it returns resolved Carve source text OR an
           error. If NO resolver is configured the directive is left LITERAL
           (the processor emits the verbatim `{{ … }}`). This mirrors the
           bibliography loader precedent (host resolves, parser stays pure;
           docs/extensions.md §6.1) and preserves browser / WASM parity.
           OPT-IN: no resolver is configured by default, and a host MUST leave
           it off for untrusted input unless it has satisfied PART 9 §25 (File
           inclusion).

        I4 MECHANISM (normative on OUTCOME, permissive on mechanism). The
           observable result MUST be AS IF the resolved content appeared inline
           at the directive site, SUBJECT TO FRAGMENT CONTAINMENT: included
           content is parsed as a SELF-CONTAINED FRAGMENT. A construct still
           open at the end of the included content CLOSES at child EOF (an
           unclosed fence or div terminates there, exactly as at the end of a
           standalone document); included content NEVER captures or
           reinterprets parent content that follows the directive. An
           implementation MAY splice source pre-parse OR parse-and-merge
           events / AST -- but ONLY where the result equals the fragment-
           containment outcome. The two diverge exactly when the child ends
           inside an unterminated construct (a naive textual splice would let
           an unclosed child fence swallow the rest of the parent); that
           result is NON-conformant. This matters doubly for `@lines:N-M`,
           whose physical slice can cut a fence or div in half: the torn
           construct is bounded to the fragment, never the parent. Source
           positions SHOULD be remapped so source-mapped hosts can attribute
           included spans to the child file (Carve's AST already distinguishes
           snippet-local from document-space offsets; carve-js src/ast.ts
           `Position`).

        I5 CROSS-FILE COLLISIONS. An identifier that expansion brings together
           is handled according to whether it is DOCUMENT-VISIBLE or FILE-LOCAL.

           DOCUMENT-VISIBLE IDENTIFIERS -- DETERMINISTIC RENAME-ON-COLLISION.
           Two namespaces are document-visible: they are exposed by the
           assembled document and can be targeted across file boundaries.
           (a) ELEMENT IDS. Every EXPLICIT id `{#id}` on ANY element -- a
               heading, any other block (paragraph, list, table, figure, code
               block, div, admonition, ...), a list item, a table row or cell,
               an inline span, link, image or other inline element -- is ONE
               namespace, because HTML has one `id` namespace per document: a
               heading `{#tip}` and a paragraph `{#tip}` collide exactly as two
               headings do.
               Names are compared EXACTLY, case included, as HTML compares
               ids: `{#Tip}` and `{#tip}` do not collide.
           (b) FOOTNOTE LABELS (footnotes are collected and numbered globally
               in the assembled document). This is a SEPARATE namespace: a
               footnote `[^tip]` never collides with an element `{#tip}`.
           ORDER is the fully expanded document read top to bottom: parent
           before child, and an earlier include before a later include. An
           occurrence is RENAMED when an EARLIER occurrence of the same name in
           the same namespace came from a DIFFERENT file inclusion (another
           file, or another inclusion of the same file); the first occurrence
           keeps its name. Duplicates that one file already held on its own are
           not created by expansion and are not this rule's business.
           EVERY RENAMED OCCURRENCE TAKES ITS OWN SUFFIX: the least `-N`
           (integer N >= 2) such that the result is used by NO explicit name of
           that namespace anywhere in the fully assembled document -- the
           parent, earlier and later includes, the child itself -- and by no
           earlier rename. A child holding a colliding `{#d}` twice therefore
           yields `d-2` and `d-3`, never two `d-2`s, and a `{#d-2}` written
           after the include pushes the rename to `d-3`. A Warning MUST be
           emitted per renamed occurrence.
           REFERENCES FOLLOW THE RENAME. A reference written in the SAME file
           inclusion as the renamed target, that resolves to that target when
           the file is read on its own, resolves to the new name instead
           (where that file held several renamed copies, to the FIRST, the
           one the file reaches when read on its own):
             - a `</#id>` cross-reference (R4);
             - a link or image destination that is exactly `#` followed by the
               id, whether written inline (`[text](#tip)`) or reached through
               that file's own reference definition (`[a]: #tip`, file-local,
               below);
             - a footnote reference `[^label]`, for a renamed footnote label.
           A reference in ANY OTHER file inclusion keeps the name as written,
           and therefore reaches the first occurrence: naming an id the parent
           also defines is how a child links to the parent's target. A
           fragment link to an id its own file does not define is never
           rewritten.

           FILE-LOCAL IDENTIFIERS -- SCOPED, NOT RENAMED. A REFERENCE-DEFINITION
           label is file-local metadata: it is resolved WITHIN the document that
           defines it, BEFORE that document is merged into its parent. Two files
           MAY therefore use the same label with different destinations, and each
           file's references resolve to its own definition -- a parent `[a][]`
           resolves to the parent's `[a]: /PARENT` and a child `[a][]` to the
           child's `[a]: /CHILD`. This is NOT a collision: it MUST NOT emit a
           Warning and MUST NOT rename anything.

           SCOPE: the include-time rename pass covers EXPLICIT element ids (on
           any element, headings included) and footnote labels ONLY.
           Auto-generated (slug) heading-id collisions are NOT part of it and
           emit no Warning; they continue to dedup via the existing heading-id
           tracker (§13, which suffixes duplicate slugs once the files are
           merged). ORDERING: the include-time explicit-id / footnote rename runs
           BEFORE the §13 slug dedup, so ids are deterministic (the explicit-id
           namespace is settled first, then §13 dedups auto-slug ids against the
           final set).

        I6 LIMITS. The processor MUST detect inclusion CYCLES (a file that
           transitively includes itself): the offending directive is left
           literal, not expanded, and a Warning is emitted. Cycle detection is
           the PRIMARY guard. As a SECONDARY DoS bound, implementations MUST
           enforce a FINITE include-depth limit; the RECOMMENDED default is at
           least 16 (a small, human-scale nesting bound, far under the structural
           MAX_NESTING = 200 of §25) and the host MAY configure it. No exact hard
           constant is pinned. A directive at greater depth is left literal with
           a Warning. Total expanded output is charged against the same per-
           render byte budget as other amplifying features -- `max(1 MB,
           8 x input length)` (§25); once exceeded, further expansion degrades
           to the literal directive with a Warning. The byte budget bounds the
           expanded OUTPUT; it does NOT bound the WORK done to produce it,
           because a target is resolved before its size is known and a document
           may carry one directive per dozen bytes. Implementations MUST
           therefore enforce a FINITE bound on RESOLVER INVOCATIONS per render;
           the RECOMMENDED default is at least 1000, and the host MAY configure
           it. A directive past the bound is left literal with a Warning and
           MUST NOT be passed to the resolver. The byte budget and the
           resolver-call bound are whole-render TOTALS that only ever grow, so
           once either is exhausted, later directives MUST be refused BEFORE
           invoking their resolver -- not resolved and then refused -- and the
           two bounds therefore limit reads as well as emitted output.

        I7 ERRORS. Unreadable path, binary / non-text content, a `#name` that
           selects nothing (I1a), BOTH selection
           mechanisms present (`#section` + a line-range, I1), block-structured
           content in INLINE position (I2), cycle, depth-exceeded,
           size-exceeded, call-bound-exceeded, and a directive reached AFTER
           byte-budget or call-bound exhaustion EACH emit a Warning and leave
           the directive LITERAL (visible and debuggable). The last two are
           never passed to the resolver at all (I6). Inclusion NEVER silently
           drops a directive. See PART 9 §25 (File inclusion) for the security
           model: parser stays pure, resolver is host-supplied and MUST enforce
           canonical path containment after symlink resolution and MUST NOT
           fetch remote URLs without an explicit allowlist. Included content
           receives the same sanitization as its parent. The processor-neutral
           vectors in `tests/include-security-conformance/` pin these security
           requirements separately from the broader include-language suite.

        I8 HEADING-LEVEL SHIFT (`@shift:N` | `@shift:auto`). A TRANSFORM option
           (I1), orthogonal to selection. `@shift` takes EITHER a SIGNED integer
           OR the literal value `auto`, NEVER both.
           LITERAL FORM. N is a SIGNED integer; every heading in the included
           content has its level increased by N (N=2: an h1 becomes an h3; a
           NEGATIVE N raises levels, N=-1: an h2 becomes an h1). An omitted
           `@shift` is `@shift:0` (no shift, the default; current behavior
           unchanged). CLAMP: the resulting level is clamped to the valid range
           [1, 6]; a shift that would push a heading below 1 or above 6 clamps to
           the bound and emits a Warning -- the heading is KEPT, never dropped.
           IDS/SLUGS UNCHANGED: `@shift` changes only the level, never a
           heading's id / slug (ids are name-based), so `</#id>` cross-references
           into shifted headings still resolve. AUTO-NUMBERING: if section
           auto-numbering (HeadingNumbers, docs/extensions.md) is enabled,
           shifted headings renumber at their NEW level as a consequence.

           AUTO FORM (`@shift:auto`). CONTEXT-RELATIVE: the processor COMPUTES
           the shift instead of taking it from the author.
           CONTEXT LEVEL C: the level of the nearest PRECEDING heading, in
           document order, that is in the directive's OWN block container or an
           ENCLOSING (ancestor) container. A heading inside a SIBLING container
           that has already CLOSED does NOT set the context. If no such heading
           exists, C = 0.
           TOP LEVEL T: the MINIMUM heading level present in the resolved
           content, AFTER any `#section` or line-range selection has been
           applied. The MINIMUM (not the first heading's level) is used so the
           included document's internal relative structure is preserved.
           COMPUTED SHIFT: N = (C + 1) - T. N is then applied EXACTLY as a
           literal shift under the rules above -- clamp to [1, 6] with a Warning
           on clamp, heading KEPT never dropped, ids / slugs unchanged so
           cross-references still resolve, and auto-numbering renumbering at the
           new level.
           NO HEADINGS: if the resolved content contains NO headings, `auto` is a
           NO-OP (N = 0) and emits NO Warning.
           NESTED INCLUDES: `auto` resolves against the document AS ASSEMBLED at
           that point, so a shift already applied by an ANCESTOR include is in
           effect when a nested `auto` is computed. Expansion proceeds
           OUTSIDE-IN, which makes the result DETERMINISTIC.
           INLINE INCLUDES: inline content cannot contain headings, so `auto` on
           an INLINE include (I2) is a no-op.
           EXAMPLE: an including document with `## Chapters` before the directive
           gives C = 2; a child starting at `# Chapter One` gives T = 1; so
           N = (2 + 1) - 1 = 2 and the child's h1 renders as an h3.

        I9a RECOGNITION SHAPE -- RUN, NOT NODE. Grammar-level recognition does
           NOT mean "one parsed text node matches the directive grammar". A
           directive's own syntax overlaps constructs the core already parses:
           `#section` is TAG syntax, `@key:value` is MENTION syntax, and a
           double-quoted path is rewritten by smart quotes, so by the time the
           include pass runs `{{ x #intro @shift:1 }}` is SEVERAL adjacent nodes.
           A processor MUST recognize a directive over a CONTIGUOUS RUN of inline
           nodes carrying only LITERAL TEXT content (text, tag, mention, and any
           other node whose source form is recoverable verbatim): reassemble the
           single nodes silently drops every directive carrying `#section` or an
           option, and every directive appearing mid-sentence. The run STOPS at
           any node that is not literal-text-shaped, so a directive interrupted
           by emphasis, a link, or a code span is NOT recognized and stays
           literal (same rule as a bare-path directive with active inline
           markers).

        I9 VERBATIM PROTECTION. An include directive is recognized ONLY where
           inline and block constructs are recognized. Inside a code span, a
           fenced or raw code block, or any other verbatim context, `{{ ... }}`
           is ordinary LITERAL text and is NEVER resolved. This is a consequence
           of Carve's general invariant that CODE IS VERBATIM -- no construct
           transforms inside a code span or code block -- of which the include
           directive is no exception. A literal `{{ ... }}` can therefore always
           be written and displayed by placing it in code.

        I10 CONTAINMENT ROOT + CANONICAL CONTAINMENT CHECK. Every resolved path
           is checked against a single CONTAINMENT ROOT.
           DEFAULT ROOT (RECOMMENDED): for FILE-BASED entry points (a CLI
           invocation on a document path, or a convert-from-file API) the root
           SHOULD default to the DIRECTORY OF THE TOP-LEVEL DOCUMENT -- a
           document path is already known, so requiring the host to name a root
           explicitly is not necessary. The root MUST NOT default to the process
           working directory (arbitrary with respect to the document, and
           possibly `/` or a home directory).
           NO PATH CONTEXT, NO DEFAULT: for STRING-INPUT APIs there is no
           document path and therefore no inferable root; a host MUST supply the
           root explicitly, otherwise inclusion stays DISABLED and directives
           remain LITERAL (I3). This preserves the opt-in posture for embedders.
           A CONFIGURED ROOT MUST BE ABSOLUTE: any other root spec is REFUSED,
           leaving inclusion DISABLED and directives LITERAL as with no root.
           The test is absoluteness, not emptiness: a whitespace-only spec is
           refused as relative, and an absolute path to a directory named with
           spaces is a valid root.
           ONE ROOT PER EXPANSION: relative include paths resolve relative to the
           INCLUDING file (I1), but containment is checked against the single
           TOP-LEVEL root. The root MUST NOT re-base per included file; a
           re-based root shrinks at every nesting level, so a nested document
           could never reference a sibling directory of the project.
           CANONICAL, NOT LEXICAL (MUST): a processor MUST canonicalize the
           candidate path (resolving symbolic links) and then verify the
           canonical result is contained within the canonical root. A `..`
           segment is PERMITTED exactly when the canonical result stays inside
           the root. A lexical `..` rejection is wrong on both sides: TOO STRICT
           (it rejects legitimate sibling-directory layouts, e.g. a document in
           `chapters/` including `../shared/glossary.crv` whose target is inside
           the project root) and TOO WEAK (symbolic links and absolute paths
           escape a root with no `..` present at all). Absolute paths remain
           DENIED unless they canonicalize inside the root. Given the layout
           `book/{main.crv, chapters/ch1.crv, shared/glossary.crv}` with root
           `book/`: from `chapters/ch1.crv`, `../shared/glossary.crv` is ALLOWED
           and `../../../etc/passwd` is DENIED. See PART 9 §25 (File inclusion)
           and docs/security.md.
           A containment refusal MUST NOT reveal whether the target exists.
           For a missing path, canonicalize the longest existing prefix and
           normalize the remaining path before checking containment. Present
           and absent targets outside the root MUST both be refused as
           containment denials, without reading or checking the existence of
           the target itself first.

        I11 DEPENDENCY REPORTING. A processor that expands includes MUST report,
           alongside the expanded document and any Warnings, the SET OF INCLUDE
           TARGETS it touched during the expansion.
           SCOPE: the set covers the ENTIRE recursive expansion, including
           nested children, DE-DUPLICATED, in a DETERMINISTIC order.
           IDENTITY: each target is identified by the resolver's CANONICAL ID
           where one is supplied -- the same identity the cycle guard uses in
           I6 -- and otherwise by the RESOLVED PATH. Two spellings that name
           the same document MUST return the same id (`a/b.crv`, `a/./b.crv`).
           An unresolved target's id is resolved against the including file
           like a resolved one, so it names where the file would appear; a
           path that escapes the containment root (I10) keeps the directive's
           spelling. So does a target that exists but the resolver refuses.
           So does a request carrying a URI scheme, which names no place.
           ATTEMPTED TARGETS ARE INCLUDED: the set MUST contain targets that
           were ATTEMPTED but NOT resolved -- missing or unreadable, binary,
           containment-denied (I10), cycle-broken (I6), and depth- or
           size-exceeded (I6).
           DISTINGUISHABILITY: each entry MUST be distinguishable as RESOLVED
           versus ATTEMPTED-BUT-UNRESOLVED.
           Hosts use this contract for invalidation and diagnostics; see
           docs/includes.md (Reported dependencies, and Editor and IDE integration).

        I12 THE WRITER PRESERVES A DIRECTIVE VERBATIM. A Carve writer MUST NOT
           escape the braces of a well-formed directive; it writes the directive
           as spelled, including one whose shape is well-formed but whose option
           is unknown or whose `#section` is not a valid identifier. Inside a
           verbatim context `{{ … }}` is not a directive (I9).

        I15 A PROCESSOR TARGETING CARVE SOURCE DOES NOT EXPAND. When the output
           target is Carve source (`fmt`, a `--carve` render, any pipeline whose
           product is Carve), the expansion pass MUST NOT run, whether or not a
           resolver is configured. Other targets expand as usual.

   20. RAW PASSTHROUGH -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-040] (governs `raw_inline` in PART 3 and the
      semantics of `raw_block` in PART 2). The BLOCK form (```=FORMAT
      fence, PART 2) emits its verbatim content UNESCAPED when FORMAT
      matches the output format and drops it otherwise; the inline form
      follows. Block and inline raw share the `=FORMAT` spelling (```=html
      / `…`{=html}). A code span whose trailing attribute block is EXACTLY
      `{=format}` (a single `=`-prefixed format name, with no other
      classes/ids/keys) is raw passthrough: the verbatim span content
      is emitted UNESCAPED when `format` matches the output format (`html`),
      and DROPPED otherwise. The `{=format}` tag is consumed, not rendered.
      A code span with any OTHER trailing `{…}` is a generic attributed code
      span (PART 3 `code_span` note), not raw inline. The corpus pins this
      (tests/corpus/50-raw-inline):
        `<br>`{=html}   -> <br>            (emitted verbatim)
        `\foo`{=latex}  ->                (dropped in HTML output)

   ============================================================================ *)
